<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/undated">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trojan War</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Trojan War was the legendary siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, told in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> and later poems. Many Greek cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> traced their founding to heroes returning from the war. Whether a real conflict lies behind it is debated, but Hittite texts about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilusa">Wilusa</a>, usually identified with Troy, show the region was contested in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trojan-war.jpg" alt="Trojan War" width="320" height="316" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sosias (potter, signed). Painting attributed to the Sosias Painter (name piece for Beazley, overriding attribution) o... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Akhilleus_Patroklos_Antikensammlung_Berlin_F2278.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
